/* ============================================================
   Ball detector — the detection engine. SINGLE SOURCE.

   This file is inlined verbatim into everflo_control_panel.html and
   everflo_image_diagnostics.html between the ENGINE:BEGIN/ENGINE:END
   markers. Edit here, never in the HTML copies, then run:

       node build_webui.mjs

   The HTML pages stay self-contained single files on purpose: they are
   opened straight from the filesystem on a phone, where relative
   subresources do not load reliably.

   The EverFlo preset was calibrated 2026-08-22 against 23 labelled images taken under the WS2812 at
   level 50 in a dark kitchen: mean error 0.030 L/min, worst 0.110 — and that
   worst is one frame whose label disagrees with its own neighbours by 0.1,
   so worst against the rest is 0.059. Margins to the quality gates: contrast
   0.173 (gate 0.10), ambiguity 15.9x (3.0), registration 0.982 (0.75),
   spread 38 (75).

   Chain: grayscale -> flatfield (box blur) -> vertical registration
          against the scale -> difference vs reference -> row profile ->
          peak + centroid -> quadratic calibration

   Everything below that is bound to one machine lives in its preset (PRESETS,
   selected with usePreset()). A recalibration (camera moved, refocused, aspect
   changed) touches that preset only:
     refs             new reference image(s), median of a labelled sweep
     CAL              quadratic y -> flow coefficients
     Y_CAL_MIN/MAX    y range the curve is trusted over (labels, or the
                      printed ticks — see the Platinum preset)
     Y_MAX_STATE      "Max" boundary above the top thick mark
     XL/XR, AX1/AX2   band x-coordinates, if the tube moved sideways
     YTOP/YBOT, BASE_TILT, LOW_*, MAX_REASON — see the preset itself
   T, the quality thresholds, is NOT in a preset: every machine shares it.
   A new calibration is checked against T, and must clear it with margin.
   Never relax T to make a new calibration "work" — the engine must say
   "no reading" rather than output a plausible wrong number. It reads
   oxygen flow for a patient.

   Code and comments are English; user-facing text stays Swedish.
   ============================================================ */

const W=480, H=640;
const SEARCH=40;           // registration search, px. Wider than SHIFT_X/Y on
                           // purpose: a big shift should be measured and reported,
                           // not silently saturate at the edge of the search.
const TILTS=[-0.052,-0.035,-0.017,0,0.017,0.026,0.035,0.044,0.052];  // tan(angle),
                           // roughly -3..+3 degrees AROUND BASE_TILT. This search
                           // finds how far the camera has tipped SINCE calibration,
                           // not the tube's standing lean, which is already baked in.
const BOXW=82, PASSES=3;   // box blur ~ gaussian sigma 41

// Quality thresholds, set with margin below the worst value in the labelled data
const T={contrast:0.10, margin:3.0, reg:0.75, spread:75};
/* How far the camera may have moved from the calibration pose, px. Sideways
   was 20 until 1.13.0, and at the care home that gate was refusing frames
   that read right: on 2026-09-30 18:17 the Platinum's camera settled 22-27 px
   to the side of its sweep (measured against the 1.13.0 references) and every
   frame after it was refused, while the ball was found where the picture
   shows it. Evidence that a SIDEWAYS move leaves the y->flow curve alone: the
   kitchen frames of 2026-09-29 (test/platinum9-kitchen-2026-09-29), 24-28 px
   to the side and 8 px down, read 2 -> 2.02, 3 -> 2.99, 4 -> 4.01, 7 -> 6.99
   against 'dag' once the gate allows it. Evidence reaches 27.8 px and no
   further, so the gate is 30, not more: the EverFlo's 34 px slide of
   2026-08-16 passed every other gate and read 1.73 for 2.0, so "sideways
   leaves the curve alone" is measured on this camera up to ~28 px, not a law.
   That frame is NOT in test/negative-old-poses/ (whose large shifts saturate
   the search at 40 with registration 0.22-0.32), so this constant is the only
   thing standing in front of it. Every negative set was re-run with the gate
   at 40 and stays refused. Vertical stays 20: dy moves the reading itself, and
   nothing has measured how far that can go. One gate for both machines, as
   for every other gate (see PRESETS). */
const SHIFT_X=30, SHIFT_Y=20;
/* How much worse a reference may register than the best one and still answer
   when the best refuses — see the fallback in analyze(). An algorithm
   constant, shared by every preset like T. */
const FALLBACK_REG_GAP=0.05;
/* How much better a tilt other than the calibrated one must register before
   the search takes it — see analyzeWith(). */
const TILT_MARGIN=0.03;

/* ---------- presets: one per concentrator model ----------
   Everything bound to ONE machine seen from ONE camera pose lives here: the
   bands, the peak window, the tube's lean, the y->flow curve, where Max and
   the bottom state begin and what they say, and the lighting references. A
   different concentrator is a different tube behind a different mount, so it
   gets its own preset instead of edits to this one — the EverFlo values stay
   exactly as measured, ready to switch back to.

   NOT here, on purpose: the algorithm and the quality gates (T, TILTS, SEARCH,
   the flatfield). Every machine shares them. A gate per preset would be a
   place to lower a threshold until a new calibration "works", which is the one
   thing this engine must never do.

   Which preset is live is decided by the caller, never by the patient: the
   device page is told by the firmware it was built with, the control panel
   asks the device, the admin page uses the preset each frame was uploaded
   under, and the Node harnesses read it from the test directory. Nothing
   analyses until usePreset() has been called — a default would be a way to
   read a Platinum frame with the EverFlo curve and never notice. */
const PRESETS={
  everflo:{
    name:'Philips EverFlo',
    XL:253, XR:285,      // the ball's travel band
    /* Anchor band (the printed scale ticks) used for vertical registration. It
       OVERLAPS the ball band by 8 px, and that is measured, not sloppiness. The
       reference is a median with no ball in it, so a ball reaching into this band
       is a mismatch that MOVES with the flow — the obvious worry, and the right
       one to have. Moving the band left to clear the ball makes every number
       worse, because it slides off the ticks and onto the tube wall and the LED
       glare, and the ticks ARE the registration signal. Measured 2026-08-22 over
       the sweep, anchor left edge 217 -> 237: registration 0.908, 0.916, 0.951,
       0.974, 0.982, 0.979 and ambiguity 7.1x, 9.6x, 11.4x, 13.5x, 14.8x, 16.3x.
       Even the dy-versus-flow drift the overlap ought to cause is SMALLEST with
       the overlap (0.09 px per 1000 rows at 233, against 0.46 with none). */
    AX1:233, AX2:261,
    /* Peak search range, in REFERENCE rows — dy is already removed when the
       difference profile is built, so this window does not move with the camera.

       It is the ball's PHYSICAL range plus clearance, not the picture's. Anything
       outside is by construction not the ball, and letting it compete for the peak
       is how the engine lost a whole afternoon twice: from 2026-08-16 13:09 and
       2026-08-17 12:51 UTC low sun put a bright edge just below the old YTOP=60
       and its clipped tail beat the real ball, while below the tube the chrome
       nut's specular highlight was the resting ball's nearest rival all along.

       Re-measured 2026-08-22 for the new camera pose and the LED. The ball now
       lives at y 147 (above the printed scale) .. 447 (its resting stop), and both
       rivals are still there:
         YBOT 462..470 is a plateau at 14.8x ambiguity; 474 drops to 10.9x.
         YTOP 95..115 sits at 14.8x and IMPROVES upward — 125 gives 15.9x, 135
           gives 18.6x — but 135 starts pulling the over-scale reading, so the
           usable range ends at 130.
       The values below sit inside both: 21 rows clear of the highest ball, 19
       clear of the resting one.

       YBOT was 466 for one night. It was chosen so the resting ball's blob is
       never clipped — and the first morning of daylight showed why that is the
       wrong priority. Daylight rebalances the auto-exposure, the LED's glow at
       the tube's foot goes darker relative to the reference, and that reads as a
       rival blob ramping through y 446..465 — THROUGH the resting ball's rows, so
       no window keeps both. Measured 2026-08-23: at dawn the rival took ambiguity
       to 1.6x and refused frames whose ball was found correctly at y=350.

       445 excludes the rival's whole ramp and costs nothing that matters
       (decided with the user 2026-08-23: flows in use are 1.5 L/min and up):
         - the sweep is unchanged to the last digit, margins identical, down to
           YBOT 440;
         - the resting ball IS clipped now — its peak reads y=440.7 instead of
           447 — but the flow that comes out is 0.20, still under the 0.3
           boundary, so a switched-off machine still says "Under 0,3";
         - dawn frames 1254/1255 (refused at 2.0-2.5x) read at 14.9-23.5x.
       Full daylight still refuses on REGISTRATION — the whole scene stops
       matching the night reference, which no peak window can fix and the reg
       gate is right to catch. That needs a second, daylight reference.

       YTOP must stay below Y_MAX_STATE (150) or the "Max" state becomes
       unreachable, and YBOT must clear the resting ball's whole blob. */
    YTOP:125, YBOT:445,
    RY1:100, RY2:460,    // row band whose column profile gives the sideways shift
    /* The tube leans, so every band follows it. Mind the sign: analysis runs on a
       MIRRORED canvas (the control panel applies scale(-1,1) before handing pixels
       over), and a mirror flips the sign of a tilt. The safe way to get this right
       is the way it was measured — in the analysis frame itself, on the saved
       images, which are already oriented and mirrored the same way. Reading the
       angle off an upright screen and typing it in is how it went wrong on
       2026-08-16, and the error doubles the misalignment instead of removing it.

       Re-measured 2026-08-22 after the camera moved and the LED went in, by two
       independent routes that agree: the ball's own path through the tube leans
       tan 0.0588 over its 306 rows of travel, and the per-frame tilt search
       reports zero deviation for all 23 frames over BASE_TILT 0.062..0.070. The
       value below is the middle of that plateau, and it matches a line drawn
       along the tube by hand in a vector editor: 3.7 degrees against 3.78 here.

       Unlike 2026-08-16 this is NOT a knife edge any more. Worst error moves from
       0.113 to 0.110 across tan 0.040..0.078, because the per-frame search
       absorbs the difference. What the constant still decides is what `tilt` MEANS
       in a reading: analyze() reports deviation from calibration, so a wrong base
       makes every frame report a permanent lean and destroys the one signal that
       says the camera has moved. That is why the plateau where all 23 frames
       report exactly zero is the criterion, not the error. */
    BASE_TILT:0.066,     // tan(3.78 degrees), in mirrored image coordinates

    CAL:[9.73929226e-06, -0.0252142395, 9.42031795],   // flow = a*y^2 + b*y + c
    Y_CAL_MIN:154, Y_CAL_MAX:421,   // y range covered by the labelled data
    // The 2026-08-22 sweep labels the whole physical range: the resting stop at
    // y=447 (reads 0.09, so "Under 0,3") up to 5.7 at the top mark, y=154, fitted
    // to within 0.06. The frame deliberately taken past the end of the scale sits
    // at y=147, so this threshold splits 147 and 154 — above the printed scale,
    // below anything the sweep put a number on.
    Y_MAX_STATE:150,
    /* The bottom state. Tested on FLOW, not on y, so it follows the curve — see
       judge(). The ball rests at y=440.7 reading 0.20 with today's CAL. */
    LOW_FLOW:0.3, LOW_LABEL:'Under 0,3',
    LOW_REASON:"Bollen ligger vid eller nära botten — flödet är under 0,3 L/min eller avstängt.",
    MAX_REASON:"Bollen står vid eller ovanför det övre tjocka strecket — flödet är över skalans sista siffra.",
    /* What the device page SHOWS: x 215..440 of the oriented frame, the meter
       alone. The LED sits left of the tube and blazes into the lens — mean
       column brightness over the 2026-08-22 sweep peaks at 175 around x 175 and
       falls off a cliff between x 210 (153) and x 220 (119) — so cropping there
       drops the glare and roughly doubles the ball on her phone. Display only:
       the analysis always gets the whole 480x640 frame. */
    VIEW_X:215, VIEW_W:225, VIEW_Y:0, VIEW_H:640,
    KNOB:null, BACKLASH:null,   // never measured on the EverFlo: her page keeps the degree buttons
    /* Lighting references in selection order; see the REF_PNG_* constants below
       the table. A function because those constants are declared after it: they
       are 400 kB of base64 and would bury every number above. */
    refs:()=>[[REF_PNG,'natt'], [REF_PNG_DAY,'dag'], [REF_PNG_EVENING,'kväll']],
  },
  /* Invacare Platinum 9 (from 2026-09). 1-9 L/min with a red line above the
     9 mark; by its own front label the machine alarms below 1.0 and loses O2
     concentration above the red line. Same principle as the EverFlo — a ball
     in a tube, the knob on top — and the same cup fits.

     Fourth camera pose, at the care home: calibrated 2026-09-30 14:21-14:26
     local (hall lamp on, test/platinum9-sweep-2026-09-30b) with a night
     reference from 14:27-14:30 (lamp off, curtains drawn, -natt). The camera
     had been moved since the morning's pose, whose two sweeps are a negative
     set now. Each number below was measured over the new sweeps.

     The camera moves when the motor turns the knob — the way the unit is
     fastened, per the operator: dx -5.8 -> +7 px from 0 to max in this sweep.
     So both references are built from frames shifted back by their own dx/dy
     (tools/align_reference.mjs, twice against the median; commands in
     CLAUDE.md), and the dx gate (SHIFT_X) is what the mount must keep inside.
     Margins by day: contrast 0.197 (gate 0.10), ambiguity 6.0x (3.0),
     registration 0.955 (0.75), spread 37 (75). The night sweep is thinner:
     ambiguity 3.6x at 4 L/min and 4.6x at 1.5 — inside her working range. */
  platinum9:{
    name:'Invacare Platinum 9',
    /* The dark body of the ball, x 306..318. Searched centre 300..330, width
       10..16, jointly with the tilt, the anchor and the window; this one has
       the widest ambiguity margin of all that refuse none (6.0x). A band at
       310..320 with the digits as anchor gets more contrast (0.251) at 4.1x. */
    XL:306, XR:318,
    /* The printed ticks left of the ball's path. */
    AX1:272, AX2:292,
    /* The ball lives in y ~190 (the red line, see Y_MAX_STATE) .. 402 (its
       resting stop). YTOP stays under Y_MAX_STATE. */
    YTOP:170, YBOT:420,
    RY1:90, RY2:480,
    /* 0.07 (4 degrees): the tube leans in this pose. 0.06 and 0.08 refuse
       none either; 0.05 and below lose ambiguity margin. Re-measure, never
       nudge. */
    BASE_TILT:0.07,
    /* Fitted to the PRINTED SCALE with a parallax that grows with height, as
       for the morning pose. The ticks, found in the reference inside the ball
       band, sit at y 188 (9), 210, 233, 251, 274, 293, 313, 336 and 359 (1);
       381 is 0.5, 403 is 0, the red line at 175.5. The engine's y sits below
       the tick by 5.7 px at 1 growing to 12 at 7 on the straight-on whole
       marks, fitted as offset = 22.780 - 0.04602*tick (rms 0.9 px; a constant
       9.2 px does worse on the labels, mean 0.14 against 0.09). The quadratic
       goes through the ticks 1..9 with the 1 mark weighted 5x (the alarm
       boundary: a clean 1 L/min reads 1.01) and lies within 0.12 of every
       tick. Against the 13 straight-on labels: mean 0.09, worst 0.33 — the
       half marks, judged by eye between ticks (2.5 +0.33, 1.5 +0.21, 6.5
       -0.19); the whole marks 1..7 read 1.01, 1.99, 3.08, 4.05, 5.02, 5.97,
       6.88. Labels from 7.5 up were read from the side (`_approx`); the one
       named 9 reads 9.00. */
    CAL:[9.96963652e-06, -0.0551701094, 19.8055070],
    Y_CAL_MIN:202, Y_CAL_MAX:365,     // the ticks 9 and 1, offset, not the labels
    /* The red line, 175.5 in tick space, is engine y 190.2 by the offset line,
       an extrapolation of ~55 px beyond the 7 mark. Above it is Max; below,
       9.0-9.67 are numbers. The sweep's `max` frame ("the ball on the red
       line", judged from the side) reads 9.43 at y 194.9, 5 px under the
       boundary. No frame has the ball clearly past the line. */
    Y_MAX_STATE:190,
    /* Under 1 the scale is stretched and this curve reads low there (the 0.5
       tick comes out at -0.02), so no honest number exists below 1: it is one
       state, "Under 1". The boundary sits at 0.8 on the curve (y 369) so a
       clean 1 L/min (reads 1.01 by day) does not flip with the ball's bob. In
       the dark sweep the resting ball is REFUSED ("Osäker") at the calibrated
       tilt — the ticks near the top rival it; 0.5 and 1 L/min were too from
       1.12.14 until the 1.13.0 references, and now read "Under 1" and 0.89.
       Her flows are 1.5 and up. By day the resting ball reads -0.76. */
    LOW_FLOW:0.8, LOW_LABEL:'Under 1',
    LOW_REASON:"Flödet är under 1 L/min — maskinen larmar för lågt flöde, eller så är den avstängd.",
    MAX_REASON:"Bollen står ovanför den röda linjen — maskinen ska inte ställas högre, syrehalten sjunker.",
    /* Her page shows the meter alone: x 240..380, y 100..520. */
    VIEW_X:240, VIEW_W:140, VIEW_Y:100, VIEW_H:420,
    /* Degrees of knob per flow, measured by "Kalibrera ratten" on the control
       panel 2026-09-29 (18:04-18:23 local, tilt locked at 0, three runs joined
       with "Fortsätt förra", step 10 degrees): [[L/min, degrees from the
       table's first row], ...], and the backlash, measured on reversing at
       8.81. Taken as the routine printed it. Two things in the raw climb,
       neither changing this table by more than 10 degrees (about 0.1 L/min)
       when smoothed away with a +-25..50 degree local fit:
       - the reading sticks for 20-40 degrees at each whole mark (4.99-5.06,
         5.97-6.05, 6.95-7.04, 8.01-8.09) and then catches up — the ball
         crossing a printed tick in the band, not the knob;
       - 4..5 L/min takes about 210 degrees where 1..4 takes 90 per L/min and
         5..6 about 140. Every smoothing keeps it, so it is the knob, not the
         reading: the valve, or the cup slipping there under more torque — the
         flow still followed every step. A second run would tell which. */
    /* SWITCHED OFF 2026-09-30 (1.12.11) by the operator: the flow-step
       buttons are gone, her page offers the fixed-degree buttons + ++ +++
       only. Too uncertain — the camera moves with the knob, and every pose
       change costs a recalibration the steps then lean on. The measured
       table, kept for if they come back (it is the machine's, not the
       camera's):
         KNOB:[[1,-4],[1.5,38],[2,95],[2.5,141],[3,182],[3.5,234],[4,277],[4.5,382],[5,487],[5.5,558],[6,627],[6.5,693],[7,794],[7.5,863],[8,944],[8.5,1094],[8.81,1145]],
         BACKLASH:21
       A null KNOB is the switch: her page shows the degree buttons with no
       line about it (knobPlan() and the flow buttons stay compiled in). */
    KNOB:null,
    /* The routine printed 35: the reading first fell 0.08 on the fourth 10°
       step back from 8.81 (40°), less half a step. But 0.08 L/min of fall is
       also ball movement — 0.29 L/min over the table's last 50°, so about
       14° — and that is not slack. 40 - 5 - 14 = 21, give or take half a step.
       The routine subtracts it itself since 1.12.4. */
    BACKLASH:null,
    /* Two references, both of this pose: 'dag' (the hall lamp on) and 'natt'
       (lamp off, curtains drawn, the WS2812 alone), the night one shifted into
       the day one's coordinates by its median's offset (dx -0.19, dy -1.28).
       What keeps each out of the other's lighting is registration alone: on
       the dark frames 'dag' passes every gate and reads LOW in 1-2 L/min (a
       true 2 as 1.66), held off only by 'natt' registering 0.07-0.09 better —
       a lighting between the two (dusk, the hall lamp at night) could let it
       answer. The reverse, near the top: 'natt' comes within 0.03-0.04 of
       'dag' on the top day frames and reads 0.13-0.17 high there. */
    /* Since 1.13.0 also three of the pose the camera settled into on
       2026-09-30 18:17, 22-27 px to the side (see SHIFT_X): 'natt2' (dark),
       'hall2' (the hall lamp) and 'dag2' (daylight), built from uploaded
       frames rather than a sweep. The two above stay: they carry the sweeps
       the curve was fitted to. The new ones also answer some frames of older
       poses — the night sweep's 0.5 and 1 L/min, six kitchen frames — all
       within tolerance, some through the fallback. */
    refs:()=>[[REF_PNG_PLATINUM9_DAG,'dag'], [REF_PNG_PLATINUM9_NATT,'natt'],
              [REF_PNG_PLATINUM9_NATT2,'natt2'], [REF_PNG_PLATINUM9_HALL2,'hall2'],
              [REF_PNG_PLATINUM9_DAG2,'dag2']],
  },
};

const REF_PNG="data:image/png;base64,";
/* Second reference: the same scene lit by DAYLIGHT. Built 2026-08-23 from 25
   uploaded frames spanning the whole daylit day, 06:52-19:52 local (ids
   1257-1268, 1270, 1310, 1322, 1331, 1336, 1338-1340, 1344, 1347, 1352,
   1354, 1355) — ball positions spread so every row is a small minority in
   the stack, and the per-pixel median erases the ball exactly the way the
   night reference's sweep does. Everything geometric (CAL, bands, tilt,
   window) is shared with the night reference; only the lighting differs.
   analyze() runs against every reference and keeps the best-registering
   one that produces a reading.

   v2 of this image, same day as v1. The first build used morning frames
   only, and by 15:30 a standing artefact stood at y=312 in the difference —
   afternoon light diverging from a morning reference — growing to 0.100 by
   17:31 and refusing frames whose ball peak ran weak. With the afternoon in
   the stack the artefact dissolves: 117 of the day's 118 uploads read, no
   reading moved more than 0.052, and the night sweep still picks the night
   reference 23/23. The one remaining refusal (id 1339, 17:31, 1.9x) is the
   worst-lit frame of the day and refuses honestly — its span value is known
   and the frames beside it read it. */
const REF_PNG_DAY="data:image/png;base64,";
/* Third reference: EVENING. Late afternoon and dusk are their own regime —
   low sun through the window, and the LED's share of the light rising as it
   fades. Built 2026-08-26 from 14 uploaded frames 16:03-19:21 local across
   two days (ids 1508-1524, 1675-1688), ball positions deliberately spread
   over y 201..437 so no row is a majority in the stack.

   It was not built for availability alone. Against the day reference the
   evening frames read HIGH as registration slid toward 0.8 — id 1516 put the
   ball at y=337 where its span neighbours sat at y=342, a five-pixel position
   error, not a calibration offset. Frames refused at the gates were right all
   along: 1519 missed registration by 0.014 with a hidden 4.75 against its
   neighbour's 4.74, and 1521 missed contrast by 0.008 with a hidden 4.05
   against 4.03. Seven of the eight refusals were confirmed against the
   pictures by the user before this went in. */
const REF_PNG_EVENING="data:image/png;base64,";
/* Platinum 9 at the care home (fourth pose), the hall lamp on: median of the
   21-frame sweep of 2026-09-30 14:21-14:26 local, each frame shifted back by
   its own dx/dy, twice (tools/align_reference.mjs --base, --geometry
   test/platinum9-sweep-2026-09-30b/ALIGN_GEOMETRY.json). */
const REF_PNG_PLATINUM9_DAG="data:image/png;base64,";
/* The same pose in the dark (lamp off, curtains drawn, WS2812 alone): the
   20-frame sweep of 14:27-14:30, aligned twice to its own median with the
   committed geometry, then --shift -0.19,-1.28 into the day reference's
   coordinates. */
const REF_PNG_PLATINUM9_NATT="data:image/png;base64,";
/* The pose of 2026-09-30 18:17 onward (1.13.0): the camera 22-27 px to the
   side of the sweep's, a few tenths of a degree turned, the lower tube about
   2 px further down than the upper — no sweep exists for it, so these are
   built from uploaded frames, ball at varied positions so the median erases
   it. Each frame is resampled by its own dx/dy against a first median (the
   way align_reference.mjs --base does), and the result shifted onto the
   PRINTED SCALE of REF_PNG_PLATINUM9_DAG: the tick rows inside the ball band,
   which is where the curve was fitted, and the tube's column profile. What is
   left after the shift is the pose's own stretch, ticks -0.9 px at the top
   and +0.9 at the bottom, about 0.04 L/min. Only even ids went in; the odd
   ones were the test (see CLAUDE.md for the numbers).
   'natt2', the dark room: uploads 6664-6732 (14), --shift 1.3,0.5. */
const REF_PNG_PLATINUM9_NATT2="data:image/png;base64,";
/* 'hall2', the hall lamp on at night: uploads 6654-6728 (9), --shift 3.1,0.5. */
const REF_PNG_PLATINUM9_HALL2="data:image/png;base64,";
/* 'dag2', daylight through the curtains: uploads 6742-6772 (10), --shift
   4.7,2.5 — its first median registered poorly against the lamp-lit 'dag'
   (0.62-0.72), which is why the offset is the largest. */
const REF_PNG_PLATINUM9_DAG2="data:image/png;base64,";

/* ---------- the live preset ---------- */
/* The active preset's values as plain bindings, so the analysis below reads XL
   and CAL exactly as it did when they were constants. The functions that do
   the work were not touched when presets came in (2026-09-26), which is what
   makes "the sweep reads identically, to the last digit" a real check of the
   move. Assigned only by usePreset(). */
const PRESET_KEYS=['XL','XR','AX1','AX2','YTOP','YBOT','RY1','RY2','BASE_TILT',
  'CAL','Y_CAL_MIN','Y_CAL_MAX','Y_MAX_STATE','LOW_FLOW','LOW_LABEL','LOW_REASON','MAX_REASON',
  'VIEW_X','VIEW_W','VIEW_Y','VIEW_H','KNOB','BACKLASH'];
let PRESET=null;
let KNOB_ERROR=null;   // why the live preset's knob table was set aside, if it was
let XL, XR, AX1, AX2, YTOP, YBOT, RY1, RY2, BASE_TILT,
    CAL, Y_CAL_MIN, Y_CAL_MAX, Y_MAX_STATE, LOW_FLOW, LOW_LABEL, LOW_REASON, MAX_REASON,
    VIEW_X, VIEW_W, VIEW_Y, VIEW_H, KNOB, BACKLASH;
/* References are built per preset and kept, so the admin page can move between
   frames from two machines without rebuilding them each time. */
const REF_CACHE={};
let REFS=[];
function usePreset(id){
  const p=Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(PRESETS,id) ? PRESETS[id] : null;
  if(!p) throw new Error('Unknown concentrator preset: '+id);
  /* Every key, and nothing but the keys: a misspelt field in a new preset would
     otherwise leave its binding undefined, and undefined compares false against
     everything — which is how a quality gate once stopped rejecting anything
     (T.kontrast, 2026-08-15). */
  const extra=Object.keys(p).filter(k=>k!=='name'&&k!=='refs'&&!PRESET_KEYS.includes(k));
  const missing=PRESET_KEYS.filter(k=>!(k in p));
  if(extra.length||missing.length)
    throw new Error('Preset '+id+': unknown '+extra.join(',')+' missing '+missing.join(','));
  /* Present is not enough once the preset claims a calibration. null compares
     as 0, so a LOW_FLOW left null would make "r.flow < LOW_FLOW" never true and
     a switched-off machine would print 0.2 instead of the bottom state — the
     same silent failure as undefined, one step removed. Only the display crop
     may stay null: the device page then shows the whole frame. */
  if(p.CAL){
    // The display crop and the knob table are optional: without them the
    // page shows the whole frame, and degree buttons.
    const unset=PRESET_KEYS.filter(k=>!k.startsWith('VIEW_')&&k!=='KNOB'&&k!=='BACKLASH'&&p[k]==null);
    if(unset.length||!p.refs().length)
      throw new Error('Preset '+id+' has a curve but is incomplete: '+
                      (unset.length?'unset '+unset.join(','):'no references'));
  }
  // A knob table turns the motor from her page: it must be whole, whether or
  // not the preset has a curve yet. At least two rows, strictly rising in both
  // flow and degrees (equal flows would divide by zero in knobDeg), and the
  // backlash that goes with it.
  // A table that is not whole disables the flow buttons, NOT the reading: a
  // table exists only to add buttons, and must never take the number away.
  KNOB_ERROR=null;
  if(p.KNOB!=null){
    const k=p.KNOB, okRows=Array.isArray(k) && k.length>=2 &&
      k.every((r,i)=>Array.isArray(r) && r.length===2 && Number.isFinite(r[0]) && Number.isFinite(r[1]) &&
                     (i===0 || (r[0]>k[i-1][0] && r[1]>k[i-1][1])));
    if(!okRows) KNOB_ERROR='Preset '+id+': KNOB must be rows [L/min, degrees], both strictly rising';
    else if(!(Number.isFinite(p.BACKLASH) && p.BACKLASH>=0)) KNOB_ERROR='Preset '+id+': KNOB without a BACKLASH';
  }
  ({XL, XR, AX1, AX2, YTOP, YBOT, RY1, RY2, BASE_TILT,
    CAL, Y_CAL_MIN, Y_CAL_MAX, Y_MAX_STATE, LOW_FLOW, LOW_LABEL, LOW_REASON, MAX_REASON,
    VIEW_X, VIEW_W, VIEW_Y, VIEW_H, KNOB, BACKLASH}=p);
  if(KNOB_ERROR){ KNOB=null; BACKLASH=null; if(typeof console!=='undefined') console.warn(KNOB_ERROR); }
  PRESET=id;
  REFS=REF_CACHE[id]||[];
}
/* ---------- flow steps on her page ----------
   How far to turn the knob to change the flow by `delta` L/min from `flow`,
   from the live preset's measured KNOB table. The target is clamped to the
   range the table covers AND to 1..9 L/min — never above 9, never below 1, so
   at 8.2 a "+2" turns only to the top. `lastOp` is the direction the knob
   was last turned, as the DEVICE reports it (any page, any phone; a turn by
   hand it cannot know): the backlash is paid only when turning the other
   way, and not when it is unknown — turning a little short is the
   safe error, turning too far is not, which is also why degrees round down.
   Returns {op, deg, target, from} or null: no table, or a flow outside what
   the table covers, is no promise at all — the page then offers the fixed
   degree buttons instead. deg can exceed one press (180°); the caller splits. */
function knobDeg(f){
  for(let i=1;i<KNOB.length;i++){
    const [f0,d0]=KNOB[i-1], [f1,d1]=KNOB[i];
    if(f>=f0 && f<=f1) return d0+(d1-d0)*(f-f0)/(f1-f0);
  }
  return null;
}
/* The inverse: the flow at `d` degrees on the table, or null off its ends. */
function knobFlowAt(d){
  for(let i=1;i<KNOB.length;i++){
    const [f0,d0]=KNOB[i-1], [f1,d1]=KNOB[i];
    if(d>=d0 && d<=d1) return f0+(f1-f0)*(d-d0)/(d1-d0);
  }
  return null;
}
/* The range the buttons act on: the measured table, inside 1..9 L/min. */
function knobRange(){
  if(!KNOB || KNOB.length<2) return null;
  return {lo:Math.max(1.0, KNOB[0][0]), hi:Math.min(9.0, KNOB[KNOB.length-1][0])};
}
/* Is `flow` something the buttons can step from? `margin` lets a page that
   is already showing the flow buttons keep them while the ball bobs just past
   an end of the table — without it the two button sets would swap every
   second at 1.0 and at the top. Past an end, knobPlan steps from the end
   itself: the turn comes out short, never long. */
function knobCovers(flow, margin){
  const r=knobRange();
  return !!r && Number.isFinite(flow) && flow>=r.lo-(margin||0) && flow<=r.hi+(margin||0);
}
function knobPlan(flow, delta, lastOp, margin){
  if(!knobCovers(flow, margin) || !Number.isFinite(delta) || delta===0) return null;
  const {lo, hi}=knobRange();
  const from=Math.min(hi, Math.max(lo, flow));
  // From the clamped start: past an end the step is sized from the end, so
  // 9.0 -0,2 turns what 8.8 -> 8.6 needs, and lands near 8.8.
  const target=Math.min(hi, Math.max(lo, from+delta));
  const op = delta>0 ? 'plus' : 'minus';
  let deg=Math.abs(knobDeg(target)-knobDeg(from));
  // Clamped to where it already is: at the limit, and nothing to turn. "Nothing"
  // includes a turn under the motor's 4° when the target IS the limit: from
  // 1.01 a "-1" is not a step too small to make, it is already at the bottom.
  if(Math.abs(target-from)<0.01 || (deg<4 && (target===lo || target===hi)))
    return {op, deg:0, target, from, lo, hi, atLimit:true};
  /* The slack, on a reversal — but only if the turn would stay inside lo..hi
     even with NO slack to take up. The device knows its own last direction,
     not a hand on the knob: turned by hand the other way, the slack is
     already taken and the extra degrees move the ball. Measured 2026-09-29
     with the 21° slack: from 1.2, "-0,2" after a hand turn down would land
     under 1, where the Platinum alarms. There the slack is left out and the
     step comes out short, which is the safe error. */
  if(lastOp && lastOp!==op && BACKLASH>0){
    const land=knobFlowAt(knobDeg(from) + (op==='plus' ? 1 : -1)*(deg+BACKLASH));
    if(land!==null && land>=lo && land<=hi) deg+=BACKLASH;
  }
  // Round down: short is the safe error. Under 4° the caller turns nothing.
  return {op, deg:Math.floor(deg), target, from, lo, hi, atLimit:false};
}

/* A preset without a curve has not been calibrated yet. */
const isCalibrated=(id)=>!!(Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(PRESETS,id)&&PRESETS[id].CAL);
/* True once the live preset's references are built (an uncalibrated preset has
   none, and is ready at once: it can only refuse). */
const refsReady=()=>PRESET!==null&&REF_CACHE[PRESET]!==undefined;

/* ---------- image helpers ---------- */
function toGray(imgData){
  const d=imgData.data, g=new Float32Array(W*H);
  for(let i=0,j=0;i<g.length;i++,j+=4)
    g[i]=0.299*d[j]+0.587*d[j+1]+0.114*d[j+2];
  return g;
}
function boxH(src,dst,r){
  const win=2*r+1;
  for(let y=0;y<H;y++){
    const o=y*W; let sum=0;
    for(let x=-r;x<=r;x++) sum+=src[o+Math.min(W-1,Math.max(0,x))];
    for(let x=0;x<W;x++){
      dst[o+x]=sum/win;
      sum-=src[o+Math.min(W-1,Math.max(0,x-r))];
      sum+=src[o+Math.min(W-1,Math.max(0,x+r+1))];
    }
  }
}
function boxV(src,dst,r){
  const win=2*r+1;
  for(let x=0;x<W;x++){
    let sum=0;
    for(let y=-r;y<=r;y++) sum+=src[Math.min(H-1,Math.max(0,y))*W+x];
    for(let y=0;y<H;y++){
      dst[y*W+x]=sum/win;
      sum-=src[Math.min(H-1,Math.max(0,y-r))*W+x];
      sum+=src[Math.min(H-1,Math.max(0,y+r+1))*W+x];
    }
  }
}
function flatfield(g){
  const r=Math.floor(BOXW/2);
  let a=Float32Array.from(g), b=new Float32Array(W*H);
  for(let p=0;p<PASSES;p++){ boxH(a,b,r); boxV(b,a,r); }
  const out=new Float32Array(W*H);
  for(let i=0;i<out.length;i++) out[i]=g[i]/Math.max(a[i],1e-6);
  return out;
}
/* The x window slides with the row, so a tilted tube is still sampled down
   its middle. tilt is tan(angle); over 550 rows 2 degrees is 19 px, enough
   to smear an untilted band across the tube wall. */
function bandProfile(ff,x1,x2,tilt){
  const p=new Float32Array(H), n=x2-x1, t=tilt||0;
  for(let y=0;y<H;y++){
    const off=Math.round(t*(y-H/2));
    let s=0;
    for(let x=x1+off;x<x2+off;x++) s+=ff[y*W+Math.min(W-1,Math.max(0,x))];
    p[y]=s/n;
  }
  return p;
}
/* Column profile of vertical EDGE energy, not mean brightness. The scale
   ticks and the tube walls are edges and stay put; mean brightness is
   dominated by shadows and reflections, which move with the light. Measured
   2026-08-16 on the same frame: edges matched the reference at 8 px with
   quality 0.90, brightness saturated the search at 60 px with quality 0.14
   and dragged the bands right off the tube. */
function colProfile(ff,y1,y2){
  const p=new Float32Array(W), n=y2-y1;
  for(let x=0;x<W;x++){
    let s=0;
    for(let y=y1+1;y<y2;y++) s+=Math.abs(ff[y*W+x]-ff[(y-1)*W+x]);
    p[x]=s/n;
  }
  return p;
}
/* Shared 1D alignment: best integer shift by cross-correlation, refined to
   sub-pixel with a parabola, plus a normalised match quality. */
function align(a,b,span,i0,i1){
  let best=-1e18, bi=0; const vals={};
  for(let d=-span;d<=span;d++){
    let s=0;
    for(let i=i0;i<i1;i++){ const j=i+d; if(j<0||j>=a.length) continue; s+=a[j]*b[i]; }
    vals[d]=s; if(s>best){ best=s; bi=d; }
  }
  let na=0,nb=0;
  for(let i=i0;i<i1;i++){ na+=a[i]*a[i]; nb+=b[i]*b[i]; }
  let d=bi;
  if(vals[bi-1]!==undefined&&vals[bi+1]!==undefined){
    const den=vals[bi-1]-2*vals[bi]+vals[bi+1];
    if(Math.abs(den)>1e-9) d=bi-0.5*(vals[bi+1]-vals[bi-1])/den;
  }
  return {shift:d, quality:best/(Math.sqrt(na*nb)+1e-9)};
}
function centre(p){ let m=0; for(const v of p) m+=v; m/=p.length;
  const q=new Float32Array(p.length); for(let i=0;i<p.length;i++) q[i]=p[i]-m; return q; }
function smooth(p,w){
  const r=(w-1)/2, out=new Float32Array(p.length);
  for(let i=0;i<p.length;i++){ let s=0;
    for(let k=-r;k<=r;k++) s+=p[Math.min(p.length-1,Math.max(0,i+k))];
    out[i]=s/w; }
  return out;
}

/* ---------- reference image ---------- */
/* One place that knows what a reference contains. loadRef() uses it in the
   browser and tools/validate_engine.mjs uses it in Node, so a new field can
   never be set in one path and forgotten in the other. It reads the LIVE
   preset's geometry, so build a preset's references with that preset live. */
function buildRef(ff){
  return {ff,
          ball:    bandProfile(ff, XL, XR, BASE_TILT),
          anchor:  centre(bandProfile(ff, AX1, AX2, BASE_TILT)),
          anchorX: centre(colProfile(ff, RY1, RY2))};
}
function loadFlat(srcPng){
  return new Promise((res,rej)=>{
    const im=new Image();
    im.onload=()=>{
      const c=document.createElement('canvas'); c.width=W; c.height=H;
      const x=c.getContext('2d',{willReadFrequently:true});
      x.drawImage(im,0,0,W,H);
      res(flatfield(toGray(x.getImageData(0,0,W,H))));
    };
    im.onerror=()=>rej(new Error('Reference image failed to decode'));
    im.src=srcPng;
  });
}
/* Builds the live preset's references, once per preset. The images decode
   asynchronously and the admin page may switch preset in the meantime, so the
   build itself runs synchronously afterwards with THIS preset live — building
   against whatever happened to be live when the last image arrived would bake
   one machine's bands into another machine's reference. */
function loadRef(){
  const id=PRESET;
  if(id===null) return Promise.reject(new Error('No concentrator preset: call usePreset() first'));
  if(REF_CACHE[id]){ REFS=REF_CACHE[id]; return Promise.resolve(); }
  const pngs=PRESETS[id].refs();
  return Promise.all(pngs.map(([png])=>loadFlat(png))).then(ffs=>{
    const live=PRESET;
    usePreset(id);
    REF_CACHE[id]=ffs.map((ff,i)=>[buildRef(ff), pngs[i][1]]);
    usePreset(live);
  });
}
/* For the Node harnesses, which have no Image: they decode the PNGs themselves,
   build with buildRef() under the live preset, and hand the list in here. */
function setRefs(list){
  if(PRESET===null) throw new Error('No concentrator preset: call usePreset() first');
  REF_CACHE[PRESET]=list; REFS=list;
}

/* ---------- analysis ---------- */
/* opts overrides what the registration would otherwise measure: {dx, tilt}.
   The control panel offers both, because a human who can see the tube beats
   a correlation objective — and when the scene has changed enough that the
   correlation finds no good match at all, it beats it by a mile. */
function analyze(imgData, opts){
  const forceTilt = opts && typeof opts.tilt==='number' ? opts.tilt : undefined;
  const forceDx   = opts && typeof opts.dx  ==='number' ? opts.dx   : undefined;
  if(PRESET===null) throw new Error('No concentrator preset: call usePreset() first');
  /* A machine with no calibration yet. Every field a page shows is present so
     nothing downstream trips on it, and judge() refuses on `uncalibrated`
     before it looks at any of them. */
  if(!CAL)
    return {uncalibrated:true, y:NaN, peak:0, margin:0, reg:0, regx:0, dy:0, dx:0,
            tilt:0, flow:NaN, spread:0, profile:new Float32Array(H),
            ref:null, fallback:false, preset:PRESET};
  if(!REFS.length) throw new Error('References not built: call loadRef() first');
  const ff=flatfield(toGray(imgData));
  /* Three lighting regimes, one geometry. The kitchen is lit by the WS2812
     alone at night, by daylight through the window by day, and by low sun
     plus a rising LED share in the evening — and a reference is bound to its
     lighting. The first daylit morning (2026-08-23) took registration from
     0.98 to 0.60 against the night reference while the ball sat plainly at
     the same y; the evenings did a quieter version of the same thing.

     So the frame is analysed against every reference and the winner is
     whichever REGISTERS best: registration is the one signal the ball
     detector downstream cannot game, which is the same reason it is the tilt
     search's objective. The result carries ref:'natt'|'dag'|'kväll' so a
     human can see which regime answered.

     Cost is one extra registration search per reference per frame — the
     flatfield, which dominates, is computed once and shared. Measured over
     482 frames (23-frame night sweep + two upload corpora): every corpus
     gains or holds, none loses a frame, and no reading moves more than
     0.10 L/min. */
  const tried=[];
  for(const [R,name] of REFS)
    tried.push([analyzeWith(R, ff, forceTilt, forceDx), name]);
  tried.sort((a,b) => b[0].reg - a[0].reg);

  /* If the best-registering reference REFUSES, ask the next best, and so on.
     Selection is unchanged for every frame that produces a reading: the best
     registration still answers, and nothing here can override a reading it
     already gave. The fallback only ever turns a refusal into a reading.

     Why it is needed. Registration is measured on the ANCHOR band — the scale
     ticks — while the ball band is 20 px away at the foot of the tube, where
     the LED sits. The two are decoupled, so a reference can match the ticks
     best by a thousandth and still be wrong about the foot. Measured at dawn
     on 2026-08-30: frame 2386 had night 0.901 against day 0.897 and read
     1.61; frame 2387, fifteen minutes later, had night 0.899 against day
     0.900 and was refused — while day refused EVERY frame in that window and
     night read all of them. A thousandth of registration decided between a
     reading and a refusal, and the losing reference had the answer.

     Each fallback still passes every gate on its own — judge() is the same
     function the caller will run — so no gate is loosened. What IS loosened is
     the system: a frame that must be refused now has to be refused three times
     instead of once, so the false-accept surface is as wide as the reference list
     (five on the Platinum since 1.13.0).

     That is measurable, and the 78 wrong-pose frames in
     test/negative-old-poses/ measure it: all 78 enter this loop, none is
     rescued, and the highest registration any non-top reference reaches is
     0.664 against the 0.75 gate. (Two of the 78 have a top reference ABOVE
     the reg gate, refused by ambiguity and spread, so for those the other two
     references really are judged on all five gates.) What that set cannot
     speak for is an occlusion or a garbage frame, which are not built yet —
     see the Testing section of CLAUDE.md.

     Measured: 2026-08-22..09-05, 40 refusals -> 7; 09-05..09-11, 26 -> 9, and
     all 9 of those are one hour with a second lamp lit. No new refusals in
     either corpus, no existing reading moved, and every rescued reading
     agrees with its press span or with the signed turn the device logged. */
  /* ...but only from a reference that registers NEARLY AS WELL. The case the
     fallback exists for is a near-tie: the dawn frames above were a
     thousandth apart, and the eight EverFlo rescues stored under engine
     e3a28634 were 0.008-0.037 apart. A reference that registers clearly worse
     is the wrong lighting, and a wrong lighting can pass every gate while
     reading a broad shadow as the ball: on 2026-09-29 at dusk, with the
     Platinum switched off and the ball at rest, 'kväll' registered 0.92-0.94
     and refused (contrast 0.07-0.10), and 'dag' at 0.84-0.86 answered 1.42-
     1.65 L/min through this loop, every gate green. Gap 0.08-0.09. A refusal
     there is right; the number was not. */
  let best=tried[0][0], bestName=tried[0][1], fallback=false;
  if(!judge(best).ok){
    for(let i=1;i<tried.length;i++){
      if(tried[0][0].reg-tried[i][0].reg > FALLBACK_REG_GAP) break;   // sorted: the rest are further off
      if(judge(tried[i][0]).ok){ best=tried[i][0]; bestName=tried[i][1]; fallback=true; break; }
    }
  }
  best.ref=bestName;
  /* Stored with the reading, not just computed: a value from a reference that
     did NOT register best is the first thing to look at when one sits a few
     hundredths off its neighbours, and that question gets asked months later
     from the admin table rather than live. cleanQuality() in the admin Worker
     whitelists both this and `ref` for exactly that. */
  best.fallback=fallback;
  best.preset=PRESET;
  return best;
}
function analyzeWith(R, ff, forceTilt, forceDx){

  // Horizontal registration FIRST. The camera slides sideways (measured 34 px
  // on 2026-08-16) and everything below samples fixed x bands, so without
  // this the anchor band lands on the housing and dy is meaningless.
  const hx=align(centre(colProfile(ff,RY1,RY2)), R.anchorX, SEARCH, 60, 420);
  const dx=forceDx!==undefined ? forceDx : hx.shift;
  const regx=hx.quality, xo=Math.round(dx);

  // Tilt, then vertical registration. Both come out of the same search: the
  // tilt that makes the scale ticks match the reference best is the one the
  // camera actually has. Registration quality is the objective on purpose —
  // it measures how well the scene matches and cannot be gamed by the ball
  // detector downstream, the way optimising for a clean peak could.
  // Everything here is relative to BASE_TILT: the search is centred on it, and
  // opts.tilt overrides the DEVIATION, not the absolute angle. So 0 means "as
  // calibrated" everywhere a tilt is shown or entered, which is the only
  // reading that tells the operator whether the camera has moved.
  let hy=null, tilt=BASE_TILT, hBase=null;
  const candidates = (typeof forceTilt==='number')
    ? [BASE_TILT+forceTilt] : TILTS.map(t=>BASE_TILT+t);
  for(const t of candidates){
    const h=align(centre(bandProfile(ff,AX1+xo,AX2+xo,t)), R.anchor, SEARCH, 40, 600);
    if(t===BASE_TILT) hBase=h;
    if(!hy || h.quality>hy.quality){ hy=h; tilt=t; }
  }
  /* The calibrated tilt unless another is CLEARLY better. The anchor is
     horizontal ticks, which pin a tilt weakly: on the Platinum the search
     stepped 1-2 degrees off for a registration gain of 0.001-0.022, the
     sheared band ran onto the ticks near the top, and a steady 3.8 L/min
     flickered to "Osäker" (2026-09-30, 16:10) — the third time in two days
     after the kitchen evening and night sweeps. A camera that has really
     tipped registers clearly better at its tilt, and still gets it.
     Only the READING moves to the base tilt: `reg` stays the best over all
     tilts, because it also ranks the references and decides whether a second
     one may answer. Taking the base tilt's lower value there let the wrong
     lighting win — night frames turned 1.25 degrees read 1.64 for a true 2
     through 'dag' (review of 1.12.14). */
  const regBest=hy.quality;
  if(typeof forceTilt!=='number' && hBase && tilt!==BASE_TILT && hy.quality-hBase.quality<TILT_MARGIN){
    hy=hBase; tilt=BASE_TILT;
  }
  const dy=hy.shift, reg=regBest;

  // difference profile, shift compensated
  const pf=bandProfile(ff,XL+xo,XR+xo,tilt);
  let d=new Float32Array(H);
  for(let y=0;y<H;y++){
    const s=y+dy, i0=Math.floor(s), f=s-i0;
    const a=pf[Math.min(H-1,Math.max(0,i0))], b=pf[Math.min(H-1,Math.max(0,i0+1))];
    d[y]=Math.max(0, R.ball[y]-(a*(1-f)+b*f));
  }
  d=smooth(d,21);
  for(let y=0;y<H;y++) if(y<YTOP||y>=YBOT) d[y]=0;

  let yp=0; for(let y=0;y<H;y++) if(d[y]>d[yp]) yp=y;
  const peak=d[yp];

  let sw=0, sy=0;
  for(let y=Math.max(0,yp-35);y<Math.min(H,yp+35);y++){
    const w=Math.max(0,d[y]-0.35*peak); sw+=w; sy+=w*y;
  }
  const ycog=sw>1e-9? sy/sw : yp;

  let p2=0; for(let y=0;y<H;y++) if(Math.abs(y-yp)>60 && d[y]>p2) p2=d[y];
  const margin=peak/Math.max(p2,1e-9);

  let spread=0; for(let y=0;y<H;y++) if(d[y]>0.3*peak) spread++;

  const flow=CAL[0]*ycog*ycog + CAL[1]*ycog + CAL[2];
  return {y:ycog, peak, margin, reg, regx, dy, dx,
          tilt: tilt-BASE_TILT,          // deviation from calibration, not absolute
          flow, spread, profile:d};
}

/* ---------- judgement (text stays Swedish: the operator reads it) ---------- */
function judge(r){
  if(r.uncalibrated)
    return {ok:false, title:"Ingen avläsning",
            reason:"Avläsningen är inte kalibrerad för den här koncentratorn än. Läs av bilden."};
  if(r.reg < T.reg)
    return {ok:false, title:"Ingen avläsning",
            reason:"Bilden matchar inte referensen. Kontrollera att kameran ser röret och att inget skymmer sikten."};
  /* Before contrast and ambiguity since 1.13.0: a camera that has moved too
     far also leaves rivals in the difference profile, and "två lika starka
     kandidater" then named the symptom instead of the cause. The registration
     test stays first — when the frame does not match at all, dx is noise. */
  if(Math.abs(r.dy) > SHIFT_Y || Math.abs(r.dx) > SHIFT_X)
    return {ok:false, title:"Ingen avläsning",
            reason:"Kameran har flyttat sig för mycket ("+r.dx.toFixed(0)+" px i sidled, "+
                   r.dy.toFixed(0)+" px i höjd). Rikta om den eller gör om kalibreringen."};
  if(r.peak < T.contrast)
    return {ok:false, title:"Ingen avläsning",
            reason:"Bollen syns inte tillräckligt tydligt. Kan bero på imma, smuts på linsen eller för svag belysning."};
  if(r.margin < T.margin)
    return {ok:false, title:"Osäker avläsning",
            reason:"Två lika starka kandidater i röret — går inte att avgöra vilken som är bollen."};
  if(r.spread > T.spread)
    return {ok:false, title:"Osäker avläsning",
            reason:"Störningen i röret är för utbredd för att vara bollen. Kontrollera att inget hänger framför röret."};
  if(r.y < Y_MAX_STATE)
    return {ok:true, maxState:true, title:null, label:"Max",
            reason:MAX_REASON};
  /* Test what the label claims. This was a threshold on y (Y_CAL_MAX+12 = 439)
     until 2026-08-17, and the bottom of the tube is exactly where the ball
     rests when the machine is off — so the only hard boundary in judge() sat
     on top of the device's most common state. Two frames of a switched-off
     concentrator, taken 15 minutes apart and identical to the eye, came out at
     y 439.0598 and 438.9691: one said "Under 0,3", the other printed 0.16.
     Nine hundredths of a pixel, which is noise, not movement.
     Worse, 0.16 should never have been printed at all — it is past Y_CAL_MAX,
     so it is extrapolated, and it means the same thing as the label it was
     alternating with.
     On flow the boundary lands at y=430.5, some 8.5 px from where the ball
     actually rests (seen at 435 and 439), which is a margin rather than a
     coin toss. The labelled 0.3 frame reads 0.355 and still prints a number,
     so the sweep keeps checking it numerically. */
  if(r.flow < LOW_FLOW)
    return {ok:true, bottomState:true, title:null, label:LOW_LABEL,
            reason:LOW_REASON};
  if(r.y < Y_CAL_MIN-2 || r.y > Y_CAL_MAX+2)
    return {ok:true, extrapolated:true, title:null,
            reason:"Bollen står utanför det kalibrerade området — värdet är extrapolerat och mindre tillförlitligt."};
  return {ok:true, extrapolated:false, title:null,
          reason:"Inom kalibrerat område."};
}
